Title: CSRF PoC for Exploiting Reflected XSS

Slide 1: Chaining CSRF and Reflected Cross-Site Scripting
A reflected XSS vulnerability can become exploitable against another user only iif:
Cross-site requests are accepted.
Authentication cookies accompany the request.
The endpoint lacks proper CSRF protection.
Browser SameSite policy allows cookies to be included.
If any of these conditions fail—for example, cookies are blocked by SameSite=Strict or a CSRF token is required—the attack chain may not succeed.

Slide 2: Reflected XSS: Why It Is Usually Not Directly Exploitable
The malicious payload is not stored on the server; it is returned only in response to the specific request that contains it.
Flow:

Unlike Stored XSS, the payload exists only for the lifetime of a single request and is not persisted on the server. This means:
The attacker cannot cause arbitrary users to execute the payload automatically.
Every victim must be induced to send a specially crafted request (such as by clicking a malicious link or submitting a crafted form).
NOTE: This is reportable for a PAA application under Insufficient output encoding

Slide 3: Attack Chain
Attacker Website
        │
        │ Auto Submit Form
        ▼
Victim Browser
        │
        │ Cookies Included
        ▼
Target Application
        │
        │ Reflects Payload
        ▼
Victim Browser
        │
        ▼
Reflected XSS Executes

Slide 4 – SameSite Cookies
SameSite is a cookie attribute designed to reduce the risk of CSRF.
It controls when browsers include cookies on cross-site requests.
Example:
Set-Cookie:
SESSION=abc123;
SameSite=Lax
SameSite Modes
SameSite
Cookies Sent?
Strict
Only same-site requests
Lax
Same-site + some top-level navigation requests
None
All requests (must also use Secure)

